  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1955-p12.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The domain requires \\(x\\ge1.\\) At \\(x=1,\\) the left side is \\(2+0=2,\\) so \\(x=1\\) works. To see that there is no other solution, note that both radicals are nondecreasing for \\(x\\ge1,\\) and \\(\\sqrt{5x-1}\\) is strictly increasing. Therefore the sum exceeds \\(2\\) for every \\(x>1.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "The real-domain restriction \\(\\sqrt{x-1}\\) gives \\(x\\ge1\\)",
        "Test the simplest endpoint before squaring the equation"
      ],
